A Generation Data Group (GDG) is a special type of file structure used within IBM z/OS mainframe operating systems.

The GDG itself—referred to as the "GDG base"—serves as a definition that specifies how many generations of a file should be retained and the minimum age a generation must reach before it is eligible for deletion.

Whenever a new generation is created, the system checks whether one or more obsolete generations should be deleted and, if necessary, performs the deletion.

The purpose of GDGs is to provide automatic versioning, allowing the file name to be specified in a generic, neutral way within JCL (Job Control Language). The dataset name (DSN) consists of the GDG base name followed by a relative generation number, where:

* (0) represents the most recent, current version;
* (-1), (-2), etc., represent progressively older generations;
* (+1) is used to create a new generation (see DD statement).

Another advantage of using GDGs is the ability to reference all existing generations simultaneously within a JCL job, without needing to know the exact number of generations currently present. To do this, the parentheses and the generation number are simply omitted when specifying the dataset in the JCL.

== Examples ==

=== Example 1: Creating a standard GDG for five versions, each at least 35 days old ===

//JOB1 JOB (12345),MSGCLASS=X,NOTIFY=SYSPROG1
//STEP1 EXEC PGM=IDCAMS
//SYSPRINT DD SYSOUT=*
//SYSIN DD *
DEFINE GDG (NAME('DB2.FULLCOPY.DSNDB04.TSTEST') LIMIT(5) SCRATCH FOR(35))
/*
//

=== Example 2: Deleting a standard GDG ===

//STEP3 EXEC PGM=IDCAMS
//SYSPRINT DD SYSOUT=*
//SYSIN DD *
DELETE DB2.FULLCOPY.DSNDB04.TSTEST GDG FORCE
/*

