Silver is a low-level programming language with high-level syntax, created by Edan Matayev for systems programming. It combines type-first declarations and brace-delimited blocks with classes, properties, and collection operations. Its toolchain provides native compilation through silverc and direct source execution through the silveri interpreter. [ref: Silver Interpreter, GitHub] Source files use the extension.sr; project manifests use.srp.

== Design ==
Silver combines native machine code and direct operating-system interfaces with syntax for classes, properties, and methods. Its library conventions place buffers, operating-system handles, and other implementation details behind public interfaces with descriptive names. Both layers are written in Silver.

The language provides classes, constructors, inheritance, nullable and union types, pattern matching, dynamic arrays, and string interpolation. Its standard-library documentation covers filesystem operations, networking, JSON, and system facilities.

=== Memory safety ===
The memory-safety specification defines a compiler pass called the Memory Guard. It requires checks for ownership, lifetimes, aliasing, initialization, and address validity before code generation, with rejection of programs whose safety cannot be established. The specification uses alias for references and fixed for address-related declarations.

== Syntax and semantics ==
Declarations place a type before a variable or function name, with optional type inference through var. Nullable types use a ? suffix, union types use forms such as <float64 | string>, and interpolated strings begin with $.

Functions declare their return type before the function name and place parameters in parentheses. A conventional program entry point is void main(). The following Hello world program writes a line of text:
void main() {
printline("Hello, world!");
}
Classes use construct for constructors, new for instance creation, and this to access the current instance. Properties can be declared with prop. This example defines a class with a property, constructor, and method:
class Greeter {
public prop string name;

construct(string name) {
this.name = name;
}

public string greet() {
return $"Hello, {this.name}!";
}
}

void main() {
Greeter greeter = new Greeter("Silver");
printline(greeter.greet());
}
Modules group declarations, while imports make declarations available to other source files. The documented control-flow constructs include conditional statements, loops, pattern matching, and exception handling with try and catch.

== Implementations ==
Silver's toolchain includes a Rust bootstrap compiler, a compiler written in Silver, and an interpreter written in Silver. New features are implemented in the bootstrap compiler first, then in the Silver compiler and interpreter.

=== Compiler ===
The compiler's x86-64 instruction encoder constructs machine instructions as bytes and resolves label relocations internally. [ref: mini_asm.sr, GitHub] The documented Windows backend produces Portable Executable files and calls Windows system DLL exports through the executable's import table.

=== Interpreter ===
The interpreter executes Silver source files without first producing a native executable. It accepts a source filename directly or through the -i option. Its documented options also include persistent bytecode and object caches.

== Licensing ==
Silver is described as having open-source and proprietary licensing. The compiler and interpreter repositories carry proprietary, all-rights-reserved notices. [ref: silverc.srp, GitHub]

